# Code Quality Rules

This file is read by the **.NET Code Quality Pack** extension
(**Tools > .NET Code Quality > Open Team Rules**). Edit it, save, then use the buttons in the Team Rules window:
**Apply to .editorconfig**, **Check custom rules** and **AI review**.

## Severity overrides

Change how strongly an existing analyzer rule is reported.
Severity: `none`, `silent`, `suggestion`, `warning` or `error`.

| Rule   | Severity | Why |
|--------|----------|-----|
| SA1101 | none     | We don't prefix calls with `this.` |
| SA1633 | none     | No file headers. |
| SA1413 | none     | Trailing commas are optional. |
| S1854  | error    | Dead assignments hide bugs. |
| CA2007 | none     | ASP.NET Core has no synchronization context. |

## EditorConfig settings

Any .editorconfig setting, applied to all C# files.

| Key | Value | Why |
|-----|-------|-----|
| csharp_style_namespace_declarations | file_scoped:warning | One namespace style across the solution. |
| csharp_prefer_braces | true:warning | Always use braces. |

## Custom rules

One `###` heading per rule, written as `ID: Title`, followed by `- key: value` lines:

- `severity`: none, suggestion, warning or error (default warning)
- `files`: which files, for example `**/Controllers/*.cs` (default all `.cs` files); several separated by commas
- `exclude`: files to skip
- `pattern`: a regular expression, or `text`: plain text to look for
- `message`: what the developer sees
- `replace`: optional automatic fix; the match is replaced by this text (`$1` inserts a captured group)
- `ignore-comments`: true (default) skips matches inside comments

### TEAM001: No DbContext in controllers
- severity: warning
- files: **/Controllers/*.cs
- pattern: \b\w*DbContext\b
- message: Controllers must go through a MediatR handler or service, not the DbContext.

### TEAM002: Use UTC time
- severity: warning
- files: **/*.cs
- text: DateTime.Now
- replace: DateTime.UtcNow
- message: Use DateTime.UtcNow so stored times don't depend on the server's time zone.

### TEAM003: No hard-coded connection strings
- severity: error
- files: **/*.cs
- exclude: **/Migrations/*.cs
- pattern: "(Server|Data Source)=[^"]+"
- message: Read connection strings from configuration, never from code.

### TEAM004: No Console.WriteLine in application code
- severity: warning
- files: **/*.cs
- exclude: **/Program.cs
- pattern: \bConsole\.WriteLine\(
- message: Use ILogger instead of Console.WriteLine.

## AI review context

Facts about your codebase, sent with every AI review so the model judges the code correctly. These are not rules.
The C# language version and target framework are detected from the project automatically.

- Primary constructors (`public class OrderController(IMediator mediator)`) are our standard way to inject dependencies.
- Controllers delegate to MediatR handlers; injecting `IMediator` is expected.

## AI review guidelines

Plain-language rules checked by **AI review**. Keep each one short and specific.

- Methods should do one thing; split methods longer than about 40 lines.
- Names must describe intent: no abbreviations like `mgr`, `tmp` or `obj`.
- Catch blocks must log the exception with context or rethrow it; never swallow exceptions silently.
- Public methods validate their arguments and throw ArgumentException or ArgumentNullException.
- Methods that return collections never return null; return an empty collection instead.
- Async methods must be awaited; never call .Result or .Wait() on a Task.
